// DF-249a, the bounds-panic wording family: `FixedBuf.set` panics out of range
// with the index and the length, and the length here is the CONST GENERIC `N` —
// a value the message reads straight out of the type, which is the one row where
// the length is not a field or a `len()` call.
//
// `set` is the family's member here because DF-294a moved `get` to the
// `None`-returning side of the accessor rule; the panicking direct accessor on
// this type is `set`, and the wording is what this test pins.
//
// `--no-hidden-alloc` is the point of pinning this one: the family renders
// through design 137's `{}` format arguments (stack scratch), never through
// interpolation, so a bounds panic still reports itself where there is no
// allocator — which is exactly where `FixedBuf` is meant to be used.
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --no-hidden-alloc
// EXPECT: panic
// EXPECT-PANIC-CONTAINS: FixedBuf.set: index out of range: 11 (len 8)

import std.fixedbuf.{FixedBuf}

func main() {
    var b = FixedBuf<8>()
    b.set(0, 65)                // in range, fine
    b.set(11, 66)               // past the end -> panic
}
